## 4. Data ownership

### Who owns which fact

| Fact | Owner | Our copy |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Login identity, emails, linked social accounts | Clerk | Only the Clerk subject on `users` |
| Entries, settings, account state | Convex | Authoritative |
| Subscription and payment status | Stripe | `subscriptions` row: a last-known mirror reconciled by Stripe webhooks, never the authority on money |
| Plan names, entitlements, limits | `packages/domain` (code) | — |
| Search text | Derived from the entry body by one builder | Stored only because the search index needs a field (an explicit derived cache) |

### Tables (Convex)

```text
users
  authSubject      Clerk subject (stable; never email)        index: by_authSubject
  timeZone         IANA zone, user-editable, default from browser
  stripeCustomerId optional
  status           active | closing
  createdAt

entries
  id               12-char Base58 public ID (packages/domain `newEntityId()`)   index: by_publicId
  ownerId          -> users                                                       index: by_owner_date (ownerId, date)
  date             local calendar date "YYYY-MM-DD" in the owner's time zone
  body             BlockNote JSON
  searchText       derived by `buildEntrySearchText(body)` on every write          searchIndex: filterFields [ownerId]
  createdAt, updatedAt
  deletedAt        optional (soft delete → trash, purged after 30 days) [Assumed]

subscriptions      one per user (one subscriber per account, per brief)
  userId, stripeSubscriptionId, status, planKey, currentPeriodEnd, updatedFromEventAt

webhookEvents      dedupe by provider event ID (Clerk + Stripe)
closedAccounts     sha256(authSubject), closedAt, origin: provider | product, expiresAt? (see §4.5)
```

**One entry per calendar day per user [Assumed].** The brief's "today's entry" and "a calendar of past entries"
both suggest this. Convex has no unique constraints, so `entries.createForDate` looks the date up through
`by_owner_date` and returns the existing entry if there is one. Two tabs racing to create today's entry end up
on the same row. URLs use the opaque `id`, not the date (see §5), so allowing several entries a day later would
not break any link.

**Back-dating:** a person can write an entry for an empty past day. Future dates are refused **[Assumed]**.

**"Today"** means today in `users.timeZone`, not UTC and not the server's clock.

### 4.1 The one authorization gate

Every query and mutation goes through two helpers in `packages/backend`. They are the only way to get at user data:

- `requireUser(ctx)`: resolves the caller's `users` row from the **verified** identity. It never accepts a user ID
  or owner ID from the client.
- `requireOwnedEntry(ctx, publicId)`: loads by `by_publicId` and returns the entry only if
  `entry.ownerId === user._id`. If the entry is missing, deleted, or **owned by someone else**, it throws the same
  `ENTRY_NOT_FOUND`. That way an entry ID can't be used to test whether an entry exists.

Capabilities still have names (`entry.read`, `entry.write`, `entry.delete`, `account.export`, `account.close`),
and the gate fails closed on an unknown one. With a single owner the rule is simply "owner, plus plan where
billing says so." The plan check (§4.3) is a separate step from the ownership check.

There is no raw entry ID handed out that skips the gate: list, calendar, and search queries all filter by the
caller's `ownerId` in the index itself. The search index has `ownerId` as a **mandatory** filter. The
`searchEntries` helper is the only call site, and it always applies that filter.

### 4.2 Privacy posture (the product is sold on this)

- **Operators can't read entries.** The admin surface, when it exists, shows account, billing, and job metadata only.
  No admin capability covers entry `body` or `searchText`. **[Assumed: the owner wants this policy. It also needs to
  be stated on `/privacy`.]**
- Entry content never goes to logs, error reporting, analytics, or third-party scripts. Error reporting scrubs
  request and response bodies from Convex functions that touch entries.
- All browser dependencies, including the BlockNote editor bundle, are served from our own origin. No runtime CDNs.
- No LLM or other third-party processing of entry content.
- Encryption: Convex's provider-managed encryption at rest. **Not end-to-end** **[Assumed]**. See open question 1.
  End-to-end encryption would make server-side search impossible as designed in §4.4.

### 4.3 Billing and what a lapsed plan means

- Stripe customer per `users` row. Checkout and the customer portal are Stripe-hosted, opened from
  `/settings/billing`.
- Stripe webhooks: verify the signature, dedupe by event ID, and update the `subscriptions` mirror. "Is this account
  entitled?" is derived at the gate from `subscriptions.status` plus plan rules in `packages/domain`. It is never
  stored as a flag on entries.
- **[Assumed] A lapsed or missing subscription never hides, locks, or deletes an entry.** Reading, searching,
  exporting, and deleting always work. Only *writing new or edited entries* needs an active subscription or trial.
  For a privacy journal, keeping someone's own words from them would be a betrayal of the pitch.
- Free tier and trial terms are undecided (open question 3). The gate is shaped so that either answer is a change to
  constants in `packages/domain`.
- Test mode on local and preview, live mode only on production.

### 4.4 Search

- Full-content search over entry text. A journal's search is useless on metadata alone, so we accept the
  obligations that come with indexing content.
- One builder, `buildEntrySearchText(body)`, flattens BlockNote JSON to normalized text. Every write and every
  future migration calls it.
- A Convex search index on `entries.searchText`, filtered by `ownerId`. Soft-deleted entries are excluded. When an
  entry is purged, its search text goes with the row, so no separate index cleanup is needed.

### 4.5 Account closure and deletion

Started from `/settings/account` ("Delete my journal") or by Clerk's `user.deleted` webhook. Both paths run one
bounded, resumable job:

1. Mark `users.status = closing`. From this point every gate treats the user as gone.
2. Cancel the Stripe subscription. Keep the Stripe customer only as long as invoices legally require.
3. Delete entries in batches, rescheduling itself until none are left.
4. Delete the `subscriptions` row and the user's `webhookEvents` dedupe rows.
5. For product-started closure: delete the Clerk user.
6. Write the `closedAccounts` record, then delete the `users` row last.

`closedAccounts` keeps a hashed subject so a token issued before closure can't quietly recreate the account.
`origin` is a required argument:

- `provider`: Clerk deleted the identity. The tombstone expires once it is safely past the longest token life.
- `product`: we closed the account while the identity could still exist. No expiry. Expiry scans only cover rows
  that have `expiresAt`.

Every step is idempotent, and webhook redelivery counts as success. Before deleting, the user is offered an export
of all entries **[Assumed: Markdown and JSON in a zip]**.

