## 6. Origins and env

`packages/domain/origins` exposes `CANONICAL_SITE_ORIGIN`, `CANONICAL_APP_ORIGIN`, the local defaults (`3000`,
`3001`), `resolveSiteOrigin()`, `resolveAppOrigin()`, and `absoluteUrl()`. These are stage-aware, so preview links
point to preview hosts. Env holds only secrets and true per-stage variance (Clerk keys, Convex URL, Stripe keys and
webhook secret, origin overrides). Key names are the same in every stage.

---

## Assumptions

1. Domain: `marrowjournal.com` (site) and `app.marrowjournal.com` (app). Nothing is registered or configured.
2. Social providers: Google and Apple. Email identifier with code / magic-link sign-in. No passwords.
3. One entry per calendar day per user. Past days can be back-filled. Future days can't.
4. "Today" uses a stored, user-editable time zone that defaults to the browser's.
5. Entry body is BlockNote rich text. No images or attachments for now. If they're added, the bytes go to private
   object storage with owner-checked signed URLs.
6. Full-content server-side search, with provider-managed encryption at rest and no end-to-end encryption.
7. Operators get no access to entry content, including through admin tools.
8. A lapsed subscription keeps read, search, export, and delete. Only writing needs an active plan.
9. Deleted entries go to a 30-day trash before they are purged.
10. Account deletion offers an export first and runs as a bounded job. Stripe billing records are kept only as
    required.
11. No workspaces or Clerk Organizations, as a direct result of the brief's "no teams, now or later."

## Open questions for the owner

1. **End-to-end encryption?** "Visible only to the person who wrote them" could be read as "not even us." Real E2E
   rules out server-side search (search would have to run on the device) and makes account recovery much harder.
   This plan assumes no E2E but an enforced no-operator-access policy. This is the biggest call to confirm.
2. **Which social providers?** Google and Apple are assumed. Adding or removing one is cheap now.
3. **Free tier or trial?** And what does a subscription unlock if nothing is metered? The gate supports any answer.
   Assumption 8 (never lock away existing entries) is the part most worth confirming.
4. **Multiple entries per day?** The URLs already allow it. The data rule and the calendar UI would change.
5. **Domain name** and whether it's registered.
